  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "An old script, renewed",
      "body": "<p>Tifinagh descends from the Libyco-Berber alphabet, carved on rocks and stones across North Africa more than two thousand years ago. The Tuareg of the Sahara kept using it, mostly for short messages, love poems and graffiti, while most other Amazigh communities came to write their languages in Arabic or Latin letters.</p>\n<p>In the 20th century activists revived it as a symbol of Amazigh identity, and in 2003 Morocco's Royal Institute of Amazigh Culture (IRCAM) set the standard taught here. Amazigh became an official language of Morocco in 2011, and Tifinagh now appears on road signs and in schools.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Shapes from geometry",
      "body": "<p>Tifinagh letters are made from a few simple parts: a circle (<span lang=\"zgh\">ⵔ</span> r, <span lang=\"zgh\">ⵙ</span> s), a cross (<span lang=\"zgh\">ⵜ</span> t), lines and dots. Related sounds often share a shape: the emphatic letters add a bar or a circle to the plain one, as in <span lang=\"zgh\">ⴷ</span> d and <span lang=\"zgh\">ⴹ</span> ḍ.</p>\n<p>The letter <span lang=\"zgh\">ⵣ</span> (yaz) stands for the word amaziɣ, a free person, and is drawn in red on the Amazigh flag.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Writing without vowels",
      "body": "<p>The old Tuareg script wrote few or no vowels, and could run in any direction. The modern standard writes the vowels a, i and u, and runs left to right. The short e, <span lang=\"zgh\">ⴻ</span>, is often left out: <span lang=\"zgh\">ⵏⴽⴽ</span> is nekk, I.</p>\n<p>Many nouns start with a- and feminine nouns start and end with t: <span lang=\"zgh\">ⴰⵎⵖⴰⵔ</span> (amɣar, old man, chief) and <span lang=\"zgh\">ⵜⴰⵎⵖⴰⵔⵜ</span> (tamɣart, woman).</p>"
    }
  ]
}
